and, greeting, or other distinct act.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Je vous aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"что ему стало стыдно за себя\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"After Hélène's father has publicly treated the couple as engaged and blessed them, Pierre and Hélène are left alone. She kisses him, and Pierre then says the socially expected declaration of love, which he experiences as embarrassingly inadequate.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Pierre weakly invokes or ratifies the role of Hélène's fiancé and prospective husband, but “Je vous aime” contains no explicit promise to marry. No distinct immediate response to this particular utterance is narrated. Their marriage a month and a half later establishes uptake and fulfilment of the broader match, but does not convert the love avowal itself into an explicit promise.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as an excerpt from Leo Tolstoy's War and Peace. The classification relies only on the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage supplies evidence of attraction and Pierre's internal claim that he loves Hélène, but also of desire, dread, social pressure, hesitation, and formulaic delivery. It therefore does not conclusively settle the depth or sincerity of his affection, nor whether Hélène separately accepts this specific verbal avowal.\"}"
